(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-1-general-microbiology-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Review of General Microbiology — Quiz 2","category":"Microbiology","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is the difference between selective and differential media?","choices":["Selective supports fastidious organisms; differential defines every ingredient","Selective allows several types of microbes to grow and displays visible differences between them; differential contains agents that inhibit some microbes and encourage others","Selective contains agents that inhibit some microbes and encourage the desired ones; differential allows several types to grow and displays visible differences between them","Selective is always synthetic; differential is always complex"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. One narrows what grows, the other makes what grows distinguishable. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which consequence does the lecture attribute to lysogeny that has direct clinical relevance?","choices":["The host bacterial cell is destroyed, releasing multiple copies of the virus","The bacterium may acquire new traits, such as the ability to make a toxin","The bacterium becomes unable to divide","The bacterium loses its plasmids"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Integration as prophage can hand the bacterium a new capability, and it also becomes immune to reinfection. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What distinguishes a synthetic (chemically defined) medium from a complex one?","choices":["Synthetic contains pure organic and inorganic compounds in an exact chemical formula; complex contains at least one ingredient that is not chemically definable","Synthetic inhibits unwanted organisms; complex encourages them","Synthetic shows visible differences between organisms; complex does not","Synthetic contains at least one ingredient that is not chemically definable; complex contains pure organic and inorganic compounds in an exact chemical formula"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. That is the whole distinction — whether every ingredient can be written as a formula. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Influenza virus binds host cells using which molecule, and to which receptor?","choices":["Hemagglutinin protein, binding sialic acid receptors on host cells","Type I fimbriae, binding mannose receptors on host cells","Panton-Valentine leucocidin, binding receptors on neutrophils","Lipid A, binding pattern recognition receptors on white blood cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives this as its example of a specific molecular interaction between a pathogen surface protein and a host cell receptor. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which three differences does the lecture give between animal virus and phage replication?","choices":["Animal viruses lack a capsid, replicate in the nucleus, and never integrate","Animal viruses bind only protein receptors, use no enzymes, and kill every host cell","The entire virion is engulfed, uncoating is required to release the genetic material, and release is by budding — which may assist acquisition of an envelope","The entire virion is engulfed, no uncoating is required at any point, and release occurs only by lysis of the host cell membrane rather than by budding"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Uncoating is driven by the difference between cytoplasmic and extracellular pH. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which internal bacterial structures does the lecture list?","choices":["A defined nuclear membrane, mitochondria, and 80S ribosomes within the cytoplasm","Cytoplasm, organelles, a nuclear area with circular chromosome, 70S ribosomes, and inclusions","Capsule, slime layer, and biofilm matrix","Flagella, pili and fimbriae"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Inclusions store products such as phosphate and oil droplets. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"When a microbial control method is applied to a bacterial population, what does the lecture say about the rate of death?","choices":["It is constant","It accelerates as the population falls","It slows as resistant organisms are selected","It varies unpredictably with population size"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. That constant rate is what makes decimal reduction time a usable measure. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"During which phase are bacteria most vulnerable to control methods, and what example does the lecture give?","choices":["Lag phase; example, refrigeration","Stationary phase; example, autoclaving","Exponential growth phase; example, penicillin","Death phase; example, ultraviolet radiation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Penicillin acts on wall synthesis, so it bites hardest while cells are actively dividing. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What causes the stationary phase?","choices":["Cells adjusting and enlarging before division begins","Adequate nutrients and favourable environment, plus removal of organic acids and pollutants, so growth continues unchecked","Intensifying limiting factors killing cells exponentially","Depleted nutrients and oxygen, plus excretion of organic acids and pollutants, so growth rate equals death rate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The balance point between division and death is what makes the curve flatten. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"How does the lecture divide the requirements for bacterial growth?","choices":["Aerobic versus anaerobic oxygen requirements only — with temperature, pH and osmotic pressure counted as chemical rather than physical","Selective versus differential","Synthetic versus complex","Chemical requirements — water, energy nutrients, vitamins, minerals, oxygen levels — and physical requirements — temperature, pH, osmotic pressure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Energy nutrients are given as carbohydrates, proteins and lipids. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which features belong to the lytic cycle rather than the lysogenic?","choices":["Integration of viral genes as a prophage, replication alongside the host cell, and immunity to reinfection","Viral genes replicated along with the host cell","Specialized transduction, in which all cells carry the same host DNA","Rapid takeover of bacterial metabolism, production of multiple virus copies, and destruction of the host cell"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lytic cycle also produces generalized transduction, in which random pieces of host DNA may be transmitted to other bacteria. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Peptidoglycan is a polymer of which alternating units?","choices":["Lipid A and core polysaccharide","N-acetyl glucosamine and N-acetyl muramic acid, crosslinked by a short peptide bridge","Teichoic acid and lipoteichoic acid, crosslinked by an outer bridge of lipopolysaccharide","Crystal violet and safranin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The crosslink runs between the terminal D-alanine and the penultimate diamino-containing amino acid. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Viroids differ from prions in which way?","choices":["Viroids are misfolded proteins; prions are nucleic acid","Viroids cause spongiform encephalopathies; prions infect plants","Viroids have a protein coat; prions do not","Viroids are short pieces of RNA; prions contain no nucleic acid at all"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Viroids have so far been identified only in plants. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A Gram-positive cell wall consists of which features?","choices":["A thick homogenous sheath of peptidoglycan 20-80 nm thick, with teichoic and lipoteichoic acid, retaining crystal violet and staining purple","An asymmetric outer bilayer of lipopolysaccharide over a periplasmic space and a thin shell of peptidoglycan, losing crystal violet and staining red","No cell wall at all, giving a pleomorphic shape","A carbohydrate-based wall with 80S ribosomes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is described as physically strong — resistant to stresses such as temperature, pH and osmotic pressure. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"How does the lecture distinguish generalized from specialized transduction?","choices":["Generalized transmits random pieces of host DNA; specialized means all cells carry the same host DNA","Generalized occurs in the lysogenic cycle; specialized in the lytic","Generalized transduction transfers plasmid DNA cell to cell; specialized takes up naked DNA","Generalized requires a pilus; specialized requires a capsule"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Generalized is listed under the lytic cycle, specialized under the lysogenic. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Crosslinking of peptidoglycan is catalyzed by transpeptidases. Why does that matter clinically?","choices":["Because transpeptidases are the receptor for bacterial conjugation","Because transpeptidases determine whether a cell retains crystal violet","Because transpeptidases are the target of beta lactam antibiotics","Because transpeptidases produce the endospore coat"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states this directly, which is what connects wall synthesis to antibiotic action. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which three mechanisms allow bacteria to acquire new genetic information?","choices":["Binary fission — division of the parent cell into two; sporulation — formation of a resistant endospore coat; and budding — release of virions through the host membrane","Adsorption, penetration and release","Lag, exponential and stationary phases","Transformation — taking up naked DNA from the environment; conjugation — cell to cell transmission of DNA from a plasmid; and transduction — transmission of genetic material by viral vector"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. All three are routes to traits such as antibiotic resistance or toxin production. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"How does the lecture distinguish aseptic or sterile condition from disinfection?","choices":["Aseptic applies to tissues; disinfection applies to inanimate objects","Aseptic uses heat; disinfection uses chemicals","Aseptic or sterile means removal of ALL forms of microbial contamination; disinfection means elimination of SOME forms","Aseptic or sterile means elimination of SOME forms of microbial contamination; disinfection means removal of ALL forms"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture adds that lowering numbers rather than total elimination may be good enough depending on circumstances. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"The lecture contrasts the two wall types as physically versus chemically strong. Which way round?","choices":["Gram-negative is physically strong — resistant to temperature, pH and osmotic pressure; Gram-positive is chemically strong — resistant to disinfectants and antibiotics","Both are described as physically strong only","Both are described as chemically strong only","Gram-positive is physically strong — resistant to temperature, pH and osmotic pressure; Gram-negative is chemically strong — resistant to disinfectants and antibiotics"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That difference follows from the thick peptidoglycan sheath versus the outer membrane barrier. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which projection from the bacterial wall provides true directional motility?","choices":["Flagellum","Pilus","Fimbriae","Glycocalyx"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The flagellum is listed as a specialized feature providing true directional motility. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A culture of roughly 4,865,321 organisms is exposed to a control method with a decimal reduction time of 10 minutes. What does the constant death rate imply about clearing it?","choices":["Each successive 10-minute interval removes a fixed NUMBER of organisms, so the count falls in equal steps each time","Nothing can be predicted, because death rate varies with population size","Each successive 10-minute interval removes 90% of whatever remains, so the count falls by an order of magnitude each time","The population reaches zero after exactly two intervals"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. From about 4.9 million, successive intervals give roughly 486,000, then 48,600, then 4,860 — which is why sterilisation is defined by probability rather than by a moment when the last organism dies. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which portion of lipopolysaccharide carries endotoxin activity, and which is used in bacterial classification?","choices":["Core polysaccharide carries endotoxin; Lipid A is used in classification","O-polysaccharide carries endotoxin; core polysaccharide is used in classification","Lipid A carries endotoxin activity; O-polysaccharide is used in classification","Both functions belong to the core polysaccharide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both attributions are given explicitly in the description of the outer membrane. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Under which environmental conditions does the lecture say influenza virus is more stable and transmissible?","choices":["High temperatures and low humidity","Low temperatures and high humidity","High temperatures and high humidity","Low temperatures and low humidity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture pairs low temperature with high humidity for influenza stability and transmission. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Protozoa may exist in two forms. What are they?","choices":["Yeast and hyphal forms","Trophozoite and endospore — the two alternating stages of the protozoan life cycle","Prophage and virion","Trophozoite — the motile feeding stage — and cyst, a dormant resistant stage"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Protozoa are also differentiated by motility: flagella, cilia, pseudopods, or nonmotile. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed as a cytopathic effect of viruses?","choices":["Cells fusing to form multinucleated cells","Formation of an endospore coat","Crosslinking of peptidoglycan","Conversion of a trophozoite to a cyst"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The full list also includes changes in size and shape, cytoplasmic and nuclear inclusion bodies, cell lysis, alteration of deoxyribonucleic acid, and transformation into cancerous cells. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What is the difference between a disinfectant and an antiseptic?","choices":["Disinfectants are chemical; antiseptics are physical","Disinfectants are used on inanimate objects; antiseptics are used on tissues","Disinfectants sterilise completely; antiseptics do not affect microbes at all","Disinfectants are naturally produced; antiseptics are synthetic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture's examples are Lysol for the disinfectant and Listerine mouthwash for the antiseptic. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"What health implications does the lecture attach to high mutation rates in viruses?","choices":["Changing surface properties may weaken or eliminate immunity from vaccination or previous exposure, and animal viruses that were host specific can cross to humans","Viruses lose the ability to replicate in host cells","Viruses become uniformly less transmissible over time","Viruses can no longer be identified by nucleic acid sequencing, and previously host-specific animal viruses lose the ability to cross to humans"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Human hosts are unlikely to have immunity to viruses crossed from animal hosts. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which health implications does the lecture give for genetic mutations in HUMAN hosts?","choices":["Acquisition of resistance to antibiotics and other control methods, the ability to produce toxins, and other virulence factors enhancing pathogenicity","Chemical or radiation exposure transforming normal cells to cancer cells, oncogenic viruses causing the same transformation, and viruses affecting immune cells","Loss of resident flora and overgrowth of pathogens","Changes to surface antigens that evade vaccination"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Human immunodeficiency virus with acquired immunodeficiency syndrome, and autoimmune disorders, are the examples given. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"How does the lecture distinguish yeasts from molds?","choices":["Yeasts are macroscopic; molds are microscopic","Yeasts are photosynthetic; molds are not","Yeasts are unicellular; molds are multicellular and filamentous","Yeasts lack a cell wall entirely; molds have a wall built of chitin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Candida albicans and Saccharomyces cerevisiae are the yeast examples; Penicillium notatum the mold. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Lipopolysaccharide in the Gram-negative outer membrane consists of which three parts?","choices":["Teichoic acid, lipoteichoic acid, and surface proteins","Lipid A, core polysaccharide, and O-polysaccharide","N-acetyl glucosamine, N-acetyl muramic acid, and a pentaglycine spacer","Crystal violet, iodine, and safranin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lipid A carries the endotoxin activity, and the O-polysaccharide is used in bacterial classification. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 34"}]};
